WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.540
DBSCAN finds clusters as dense regions.

2
00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.842
A point is core if at least a

3
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.738
minimum number of points, itself included

4
00:00:09.738 --> 00:00:11.690
lie within distance epsilon.

5
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.747
A chain of core points

6
00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:15.750
each step at most epsilon

7
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.160
forms one cluster.

8
00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.632
A point that is not core but lies within epsilon

9
00:00:20.697 --> 00:00:22.909
of a core point is a border point

10
00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:24.470
and the rest are noise.

11
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.125
Done naively

12
00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:29.940
DBSCAN compares every pair of points.

13
00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:33.939
Parallel DBSCAN, from SIGMOD 2020, is exact

14
00:00:33.939 --> 00:00:37.090
and work-efficient in two dimensions.

15
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.871
Our toy has eighty points in three

16
00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:41.489
clusters and some noise

17
00:00:41.554 --> 00:00:43.301
with epsilon zero point six

18
00:00:43.366 --> 00:00:44.790
and a minimum of four.

19
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.563
The plain algorithm finds sixty core

20
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.380
points and three clusters.

21
00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.234
It needs six thousand four hundred distance

22
00:00:52.292 --> 00:00:54.039
tests to find the core points

23
00:00:54.098 --> 00:00:56.544
and one thousand three hundred eighty-five

24
00:00:56.602 --> 00:00:57.650
more to link them.

25
00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.636
The paper puts the points in cells

26
00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:03.750
of side epsilon over root d, here root two.

27
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.441
A cell's diagonal is then epsilon

28
00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:09.540
so any two points in one cell are neighbors.

29
00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.054
The task removed the next step

30
00:01:12.054 --> 00:01:13.730
linking the core cells.

31
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.189
Two core cells join when some core

32
00:01:16.251 --> 00:01:18.299
point of one is within epsilon of

33
00:01:18.361 --> 00:01:20.160
some core point of the other.

34
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.894
Our version checks nearby core cells

35
00:01:22.894 --> 00:01:24.892
stops at the first close pair

36
00:01:24.892 --> 00:01:27.470
and keeps the clusters in a union-find.

37
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.715
One hundred eighty-nine tests

38
00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:32.304
instead of thirteen hundred eighty-five

39
00:01:32.304 --> 00:01:33.820
link the same clusters.

40
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.209
The agent returns a cluster

41
00:01:36.276 --> 00:01:37.884
id for every core point

42
00:01:37.884 --> 00:01:39.760
and minus one for the rest.

43
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.062
Each submission clusters twelve

44
00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:43.888
point sets on eight threads

45
00:01:43.888 --> 00:01:45.903
and must match an exact oracle

46
00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:46.910
point by point.

47
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.895
To pass, it must be strictly faster than

48
00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:51.740
the reference on every set.

49
00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.790
The score compares the geometric-mean time of

50
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.250
the small sets with twenty milliseconds

51
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.290
twenty milliseconds scores fifty.

52
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.270
Six of sixteen models passed.

53
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.982
The two best scores failed: Gemini 4 Argon

54
00:02:05.982 --> 00:02:07.234
in Antigravity

55
00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:10.205
and GLM 5.3 were each faster than the

56
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:13.020
reference on eleven of twelve sets.

57
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.090
Claude Opus 5.5 did pass.

58
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.482
It used a lock-free union-find

59
00:02:18.482 --> 00:02:20.920
and checked the nearest cells first.

60
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.650
Its own idea was a trie

61
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.030
over cell coordinates

62
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.550
which skips whole groups of cells already

63
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.470
in the querying cell's cluster.

64
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.410
Now a puzzle.

65
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.125
GLM 5.3 Flash scored sixty-eight

66
00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:34.086
point eight

67
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.599
below the reference's seventy-one

68
00:02:36.673 --> 00:02:37.560
point seven.

69
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.550
Pause and predict: did it pass?

70
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:44.980
It did.

71
00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.907
The reference is timed again in every run

72
00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:50.116
and in that run it took fifty-three

73
00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:51.650
point four milliseconds.

74
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.400
GLM 5.3 Flash was faster on all twelve sets.

75
00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.967
So: cells of side epsilon over root

76
00:03:00.038 --> 00:03:02.028
d spare most distance tests

77
00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:04.373
a union-find over the core cells

78
00:03:04.444 --> 00:03:06.220
gives the exact clusters

79
00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.920
and a pass is decided in its own run.

80
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.287
As an exercise

81
00:03:10.287 --> 00:03:12.523
double epsilon to one point two

82
00:03:12.523 --> 00:03:14.150
and count the clusters.

83
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.100
Next, Hygra.
